Article 2
Protection of Women and Children is carried out with due regard to religion, customs, social culture of the Community by prioritizing the basic principles of women's rights and children's rights.
The basic principles of Protection of women's rights and children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) include: non-discrimination;
the best interests of Women and Children;
the right to life, survival, and development;
respect for the opinion of Children;
legal certainty (Protection of victims' rights);
local wisdom;
justice and gender equality;
transparency;
accountability; and sustainability.

Article 3
The Implementation of Protection of Women and Children aims to: provide guidance to the Regency Government in planning policies and strategies for the Protection of Women and Children;
prevent the occurrence of all forms of Violence against Women and Children as well as violations of the rights of Women and Children;
protect and provide services to Women and Children Victims of Violence;
strengthen the protective environment for Women and Children from all forms of Violence, exploitation, abuse and neglect;
increase empowerment of Women and Children; and increase the role of government institutions, business entities, media and/or NGOs in the Region.

Article 10
Every Woman has the right to:
a. 	live, sustain life and improve her standard of living;
b. 	live peacefully, safely, in peace, happily, prosperously both physically and spiritually;
c. 	enjoy a good and healthy living environment;
d. 	live in a family within the bonds of a lawful marriage based on the provisions of laws and regulations;
e. 	obtain work in accordance with her abilities, requirements, and proper and fair wages;
f. 	specifically for Women with disabilities, the right to obtain facilities and special treatment in public services;
g. 	play an active role in the field of politics and government in accordance with her potential and abilities;
h. 	obtain legal information and services;
i. 	obtain services to increase her income; and
j. 	obtain other rights in accordance with her human dignity and based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 13
The Implementation of the Protection of Women and Children includes:
a. prevention and protection;
b. handling and recovery of victims of violence;
c. rehabilitation, reintegration and empowerment;
d. law enforcement services and legal assistance;
e. facilitation of juvenile justice; and
f. cultivation of a gender-just social system.

Article 8
(1) Families and parents have obligations and responsibilities toward Children in the form of: caring for, maintaining, educating, and protecting Children;
preventing the occurrence of marriage at an early age;
ensuring the continuity of Children's education in accordance with the Children's abilities, talents and interests; and reporting every birth of a Child to the authorized agency that conducts birth registration.
(2) In the event that parents do not exist or their whereabouts are unknown or due to a certain reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family, which shall be implemented in accordance with the applicable laws and regulations.

Article 14
(1) Related SKPD that oversees the Protection of Women and Children shall conduct prevention and Protection in the form of:
a. dissemination of information;
b. developing data centers;
c. conducting education on the prevention of Violence against Women and Children;
d. developing child-friendly school models;
e. encouraging increased participation of Children in preventing Violence against Children as well as socialization and promotion of Children's rights through the establishment of Children's Forums/Children's Councils;
f. formulating a code of ethics for educators and orphanage officers as well as parties involved in handling Children;
g. providing guidelines or SOPs on handling Violence against Children;
h. formulating guidelines for the implementation of recovery and social Reintegration for Child victims of Violence;
i. formulating guidelines for the role of the Community in providing recovery and Reintegration services;
j. involving the participation of the Community in recovery and Reintegration services for Child victims of Violence;
k. raising awareness about safe sex, dangers of cigarettes, alcoholic beverages, narcotics and psychotropic substance abuse, Child marriage, pornography, pornographic acts and HIV/AIDS to families, the Community and Children;
l. increasing the capacity of families and Child care Institutions regarding Child Protection and child-rearing skills;
m. increasing the capacity of all Child Protection services;
n. guarantee of continuity of character-based education in accordance with the 12 (twelve) year compulsory education program;
o. fulfillment of the right to legal identity for Children and Women including birth certificates and marriage certificates;
p. guarantee of obtaining standard health services;
q. guarantee of the right of Children to receive exclusive breastfeeding with the obligation of public spaces to provide breastfeeding corners;
r. guarantee of Protection and fulfillment of Children's rights from the womb and Women's rights;
s. fulfillment of Children's needs according to the age of the Child;
t. Assistance and reproductive and psychological health services for adolescent Children and Women;
u. development of Marriage Age Maturation Programs;
v. strengthening the function of Institutions or non-governmental organizations engaged in Child empowerment; and
w. encouraging and facilitating the development of Child-Friendly Regencies.
(2) Implementation of prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) may cooperate with Educational Institutions, Religious Institutions, Media, Business Entities and/or NGOs.

Article 6
The Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility to:
a. respect and guarantee the human rights of every Woman and Child without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, group, gender, ethnic group, culture, and language, the legal status of the Child, the birth order of the Child, and physical and/or mental condition;
b. provide support for facilities and infrastructure in the Implementation of Protection of Women and Children, including providing breastfeeding places for Children, playgrounds, recreational areas, shelters, as well as establishing integrated service centers for the Protection of Women and Children;
c. supervise the Implementation of Protection of Women and Children;
d. guarantee the availability of a safe and comfortable environment for the optimal growth and development of Children;
e. allocate budget to Institutions that have programs in the field of Protection of Women and Children;
f. coordinate programs, budgets and services that ensure ease of service to victims;
g. formulate guidelines for the implementation of Protection of Women and Children;
h. provide services for Women and Child Victims of Violence;
i. monitor, supervise and evaluate the Implementation of Protection and Assistance for Women and Children;
j. prevent, reduce risks, and carry out handling of all forms of Violence, exploitation, maltreatment, Children in conflict with the law and Children in emergency situations;
k. strengthen family-based and Community-based Child care;
l. increase Child participation in policy-making that affects them, including in family life, education and Community; and
m. increase Community participation in the Protection of Women and Children including prevention, risk reduction and handling.
The provision of facilities and infrastructure as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, is for the fulfillment of service standards;
The provision of facilities and infrastructure by the Regional Government is carried out in stages adjusted to the financial capacity of the Region.

Article 22
The Regional Government is responsible for efforts to protect Women and Children from acts of Violence that occur in the forms of:
a. Physical violence;
b. Sexual violence;
c. Economic violence;
d. Social violence;
e. Psychological violence;
f. domestic neglect;
g. coercion or deprivation of liberty; and
h. threats of certain actions.

Article 24
(1) 	The Regional Government carries out the prevention of Violence against Women and Children by means of:
a. 	disseminating laws and regulations;
b. 	providing counseling/guidance;
c. 	providing education on the dangers of domestic Violence;
d. 	conducting seminars/workshops or the like;
e. 	forming work networks in efforts to prevent Violence;
f. 	conducting coordination, integration, and synchronization of Violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
g. 	forming a Violence prevention system, mapping locations or areas prone to the occurrence of Violence; and
h. 	increasing Community awareness in behaving in accordance with religious norms and decency.
(2) 	In addition to the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) must be implemented by:
a. 	family and/or closest relatives;
b. 	Community;
c. 	educational institutions;
d. 	health institutions;
e. 	social and community organizations; and
f. 	the business sector and other institutions.

Article 7
The Community has the obligation and responsibility for the Protection of Women and Children through Community participation activities in the Implementation of Protection of Women and Children in the form of: providing information and/or reporting every occurrence of vulnerability risk and Violence that comes to their knowledge;
facilitating deliberation to reach consensus for the creation of restorative justice for Children in conflict with the law through informal channels and implemented at the village/urban village government level and institutional authority;
identifying/early detection and initial handling of Children at risk;
mediation and guidance for families experiencing Violence in the household, household conflicts, and divorce;
preparation of families and the Community in receiving Children who are victims of maltreatment and/or Children at risk; and the process of repatriation, social Rehabilitation, and social Reintegration.

